## PR Type

- [x] Other: Performance

## Current behavior

`FormGroup.setValue()` calls `assertControlPresent()` for each supplied field. Even when the control exists, this helper first calls `Object.keys(controls)` to check whether the group is empty. Updating N controls enumerates N control names N times and allocates N temporary arrays. This work also runs in production.

## New behavior

An enumerable own control proves both that the group is nonempty and that the requested control exists. Return immediately in that case, using `Object.prototype.propertyIsEnumerable.call()` so control names cannot shadow the method. The common presence-check path becomes O(N) across the group instead of O(N²).

Keep the original path for missing and non-enumerable controls and for FormArray. In particular, an empty group still throws NO_CONTROLS rather than MISSING_CONTROL. Do not cache the control map or its size: changes made by synchronous child callbacks remain visible on the next field. Value writes, validation, event ordering and parent updates keep their existing synchronous behavior.

Add regression coverage for bounded enumeration, a control named `propertyIsEnumerable`, non-enumerable controls, and removal of all controls from a child `valueChanges` callback. The enumeration test fails on the base implementation (132 calls for 128 controls).

## Production browser measurement

Compared two Angular 22.2.3 demos using full AOT compilation, the Angular linker, esbuild minification, `ngDevMode=false` and `ngJitMode=false`. The patched build replaces only this helper in the published Forms module; its baseline helper has the same logic as current main. Chrome 155/Linux, no CPU throttling requested.

Each page renders a reactive form with actual native inputs and `Validators.required`. The measured interval is synchronous `FormGroup.setValue()`, including native input writes, validation and default event emission. Form creation, payload preparation, correctness assertions, paint and later change detection are outside timing. Values alternate between two prepared records. Every native input value and control value is checked immediately after every call, before any await.

Medians of 11 measured pairs after four warmup pairs per size, alternating baseline/patched execution order. Repeated the complete run after reloading both builds:

| Inputs | First baseline | First patched | Repeat baseline | Repeat patched |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 50 | 0.8 ms | 0.7 ms | 0.9 ms | 0.9 ms |
| 200 | 2.4 ms | 2.5 ms | 2.6 ms | 2.4 ms |
| 500 | 5.8 ms | 6.1 ms | 5.9 ms | 5.3 ms |
| 1,000 | 12.2 ms | 11.8 ms | 11.3 ms | 10.2 ms |
| 2,000 | 109.0 ms | 22.0 ms | 99.8 ms | 21.4 ms |

The large-group result is reproducible in this demo; small groups show little or no useful gain. Object representation and enumeration costs depend on the JS engine, so the scaling is not smooth. These are operation timings for a production-built demo, not whole-application or INP measurements.

<details>
<summary>Minimal production reproduction</summary>

Build this component in AOT production mode and compare the base branch with this helper change. Wait for initial rendering and measure repeated clicks after warmup.

```ts
import {Component} from '@angular/core';
import {FormControl, FormGroup, ReactiveFormsModule, Validators} from '@angular/forms';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  imports: [ReactiveFormsModule],
  template: `
    <button (click)="restore()">Restore form</button>
    <form [formGroup]="form">
      @for (name of names; track name) {
        <input [formControlName]="name" />
      }
    </form>
  `,
})
export class App {
  readonly names = Array.from({length: 2000}, (_, i) => `field_${i}`);
  readonly form = new FormGroup(Object.fromEntries(this.names.map(name => [
    name, new FormControl('', {nonNullable: true, validators: Validators.required}),
  ])));
  readonly values = [0, 1].map(sample => Object.fromEntries(
    this.names.map(name => [name, `restored_${sample}_${name}`]),
  ));
  private turn = 0;

  restore() {
    const value = this.values[this.turn++ % 2];
    const start = performance.now();
    this.form.setValue(value);
    console.log(performance.now() - start);
    document.querySelectorAll('input').forEach((input, i) => {
      console.assert(input.value === value[this.names[i]]);
    });
  }
}
```

</details>

