### Which @angular/* package(s) are the source of the bug?

router

### Is this a regression?

No

### Description

When a navigation is cancelled or fails after its URL has already been committed, `NavigationStateManager.cancel()` puts the previous URL back with `navigation.navigate(previousUrl, {history: 'replace', info: {ɵrouterInfo: {intercept: false}}})`. `handleNavigate` then returns early for that event without intercepting it. A navigation that nobody intercepts is a regular one, so the browser loads the previous URL as a new document and the whole app restarts.

I could reproduce it in these cases, all with the default `canceledNavigationResolution: 'replace'`:

- Chrome, default config: a routed component throws in its constructor (an `inject()` without a provider, for example). In `'deferred'` mode the URL is committed right before activation, so the error goes through the rollback.
- Chrome with `urlUpdateStrategy: 'eager'`: any guard that rejects.
- Safari 26.6, which doesn't support `precommitHandler`, so the URL is committed as soon as the event is intercepted: a guard that rejects asynchronously after a plain link click, the back button on a page whose `canDeactivate` asks for confirmation and gets "no", and stopping a navigation in progress (`window.stop()`).

The `canDeactivate` case is the worst one: the guard is there to keep unsaved changes, and the reload throws them away.

With the History API integration, the same rollback is a `replaceState()` and the page stays. The router's tests expect that for both integrations ("should dispatch NavigationError after the url has been reset back" and "can renavigate to throwing component" run with the Navigation API too). They pass there only because `FakeNavigation` never loads a new document.

With `canceledNavigationResolution: 'computed'`, rollbacks after a push navigation use `traverseTo()` instead, which stays in the same document, so most of these cases don't reload there.

#71597 intercepts the rollback navigation, without a handler, so only the URL changes.

### Please provide a link to a minimal reproduction of the bug

No hosted reproduction, but this is self-contained:

```ts
import {Component} from '@angular/core';
import {bootstrapApplication} from '@angular/platform-browser';
import {
  RouterLink,
  RouterOutlet,
  provideRouter,
  withExperimentalPlatformNavigation,
} from '@angular/router';

@Component({template: ''})
class Home {}

@Component({template: ''})
class Broken {
  constructor() {
    throw new Error('Broken');
  }
}

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  imports: [RouterLink, RouterOutlet],
  template: `
    <p>Loaded at {{ loadedAt }}</p>
    <a routerLink="/broken">Go to a broken page</a>
    <router-outlet />
  `,
})
class App {
  loadedAt = new Date().toLocaleTimeString();
}

bootstrapApplication(App, {
  providers: [
    provideRouter(
      [
        {path: '', component: Home},
        {path: 'broken', component: Broken},
      ],
      withExperimentalPlatformNavigation(),
    ),
  ],
});
```

In Chrome, click the link. The error is logged, then the page reloads: "Loaded at" changes and the server gets a new request for `/`. Without `withExperimentalPlatformNavigation()`, the URL goes back to `/` and the page stays as it is.

### Please provide the exception or error you saw

```true
No error from the rollback itself, the page just reloads.
```

